{
  "_readme": [
    "schedule.json — the declarative job manifest for the merchandise-return engine's clock.",
    "",
    "The engine is a set of modules that only produce value when something runs them while",
    "nobody is watching. This file is that something's instruction sheet: it names every job,",
    "when it runs, which engine modules it runs, which environment it needs, and what a human",
    "should be told about the result.",
    "",
    "scheduler.py reads this file and installs the jobs on whatever host it is running on",
    "(cron, systemd timers, Windows Task Scheduler, or its own foreground loop).",
    "",
    "WHY THIS FILE SHIPS AS *.json.example: the packager's file whitelist ships '*.json.example'.",
    "It is a real, complete, working manifest, not a stub — scheduler.py loads it directly as the",
    "built-in default. Copy it to 'schedule.json' (same directory, or your cwd, or point",
    "$MER_SCHEDULE at it) only when you want to change something; your copy then wins.",
    "",
    "NOTHING HOST-SPECIFIC LIVES HERE. No interpreter path, no scripts directory, no container",
    "name, no log directory, no user identity. Those come from scheduler config (see 'config' in",
    "scheduler.py --help). This file describes the WORK; config describes the MACHINE."
  ],

  "version": 1,

  "defaults": {
    "log_max_lines": 500,
    "schedule_timezone": "host local time",
    "_schedule_note": [
      "Every 'schedule' below is a 5-field cron expression interpreted in the HOST'S LOCAL TIME",
      "ZONE, because that is what cron, systemd and Task Scheduler all do by default and a clock",
      "that silently disagrees with the wall clock is worse than no clock. The times were chosen",
      "as business-hours-local: an alert is only worth as much as the correction that follows it,",
      "and corrections happen while a human is awake."
    ]
  },

  "jobs": [
    {
      "name": "mer-engine",
      "schedule": "0 8-17 * * 1-5",
      "schedule_human": "hourly, 08:00-17:00, Mon-Fri (local)",
      "description": "The inbound->action loop: read new mail, classify, draft, then process the veto queue.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "This is the engine proper. Vendor mail arrives on the vendor's schedule, not the user's,",
        "and every tier deadline in the ladder runs against the clock from the moment a reply lands.",
        "Business hours only: a draft queued at 3am cannot be vetoed by a sleeping human, and the",
        "veto window is the only thing standing between a machine-written letter and a real vendor."
      ],
      "env_from_file": [
        "MULTICA_TOKEN",
        "MULTICA_WORKSPACE_ID",
        "MULTICA_SERVER_URL",
        "NANOGPT_API_KEY",
        "MINIMAX_API_KEY"
      ],
      "env": {
        "MER_ENGINE_SEND": "test",
        "MER_ENGINE_WINDOW_HOURS": "3"
      },
      "env_notes": [
        "MER_ENGINE_SEND ships as 'test' ON PURPOSE. In test mode every drafted reply is redirected",
        "to your own mailbox instead of the vendor's, so a fresh install cannot send a stranger's",
        "vendor a machine-written letter on day one. Watch the drafts for a week; when you trust",
        "them, change this to 'live'. 'off' drafts nothing at all.",
        "MER_ENGINE_WINDOW_HOURS is the veto window: how long a queued send sits before it goes out.",
        "Cancel one with: send_queue.py --veto <id>."
      ],
      "log": "mer_engine.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "mer_hotpath.py", "args": ["--force"], "output": "log" },
        { "module": "send_queue.py", "args": ["--process"], "output": "log" }
      ],
      "step_notes": [
        "The first step runs mer_engine via mer_hotpath --force rather than calling mer_engine.py",
        "directly. --force runs exactly the same command, but takes the shared lock first, so this",
        "hourly job and the every-minute mer-hotpath job can never classify the same reply at once.",
        "That matters more than it looks: two concurrent runs produce two different LLM drafts, so",
        "two different body fingerprints, and idempotency.reserve() would permit BOTH. The lock is",
        "what makes the pair safe -- do not revert this step while mer-hotpath is scheduled.",
        "send_queue.py --process stays HERE and only here. It is the only thing that drains the",
        "queue, so a draft queued at 3am physically cannot leave before business hours."
      ],
      "stdout": { "mode": "silent" }
    },

    {
      "name": "mer-hotpath",
      "schedule": "* * * * *",
      "schedule_human": "every minute (local), 24/7",
      "description": "Event-driven hot path: watch the mailbox cursor and fire the engine the moment a reply lands.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "The hourly engine job means a vendor reply can sit unnoticed for an hour, and from Friday",
        "evening to Monday morning for roughly sixty. Every tier deadline in the ladder is already",
        "running by then. This job costs one Gmail API call a minute while the mailbox is quiet and",
        "does nothing else; when the cursor moves and a message was genuinely added to the inbox, it",
        "dispatches the SAME mer_engine.py the hourly job runs. It is a trigger, not a second engine.",
        "It runs 24/7 on purpose and is still safe overnight, because it never drains the send queue:",
        "send_queue.py --process stays on the business-hours mer-engine job, so a draft queued at 3am",
        "cannot go out before a human is awake to veto it."
      ],
      "env_from_file": [
        "MULTICA_TOKEN",
        "MULTICA_WORKSPACE_ID",
        "MULTICA_SERVER_URL",
        "NANOGPT_API_KEY",
        "MINIMAX_API_KEY"
      ],
      "env": {
        "MER_ENGINE_SEND": "test",
        "MER_ENGINE_WINDOW_HOURS": "3"
      },
      "env_notes": [
        "MER_ENGINE_SEND must match the mer-engine job -- this dispatches that same module and",
        "deliberately never sets the send gate itself.",
        "MER_HOTPATH_MIN_INTERVAL / MAX_INTERVAL / BACKOFF only affect --run-forever, not this job.",
        "On a host with no minute-granularity cron, drop this job and run",
        "'mer_hotpath.py --run-forever' under a supervisor instead."
      ],
      "log": "mer_hotpath.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "mer_hotpath.py", "args": [], "output": "log" }
      ],
      "stdout": { "mode": "silent" }
    },

    {
      "name": "mer-case-tick",
      "schedule": "0 9 * * *",
      "schedule_human": "daily at 09:00 (local)",
      "description": "The daily case tick: walk every open case, compute what is due, surface only what needs a human.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "Deadlines pass whether or not any mail arrives. The tick is the only job that fires on the",
        "mere passage of time, so it is what keeps a silent case from quietly aging out. Once a day",
        "is the right sampling rate for quantities measured in days."
      ],
      "env_from_file": [
        "MULTICA_TOKEN",
        "MULTICA_WORKSPACE_ID",
        "MULTICA_SERVER_URL",
        "NANOGPT_API_KEY",
        "MINIMAX_API_KEY"
      ],
      "env": {
        "MER_ENGINE_SEND": "test",
        "MER_ENGINE_WINDOW_HOURS": "3"
      },
      "log": "case_tick.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "case_tick.py", "args": ["--live"], "output": "log+match" }
      ],
      "stdout": {
        "mode": "match",
        "match": "\\[(DUE|OVERDUE|NUDGE|NEEDS-YES)",
        "header": "Case tick - cases needing action:",
        "footer": "(full board: your Multica workspace)"
      }
    },

    {
      "name": "mer-calendar-sync",
      "schedule": "30 9 * * *",
      "schedule_human": "daily at 09:30 (local)",
      "description": "Mirror each open case's phase deadline onto its due date, then upsert one all-day calendar event per dated case.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "Two steps, in order, because the calendar reads the due date the first step writes.",
        "This is the only job that always speaks: it prints a one-line summary every run so the",
        "clock itself is auditable. Everything else here is silent-when-healthy, and a set of jobs",
        "that are all silent gives you no way to tell 'nothing wrong' from 'nothing running'."
      ],
      "env_from_file": ["MULTICA_TOKEN", "MULTICA_WORKSPACE_ID", "MULTICA_SERVER_URL"],
      "env": {},
      "log": "calendar_sync.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "sync_deadlines.py", "args": ["--live"], "output": "log+match" },
        { "module": "multica_calendar_sync.py", "args": [], "output": "log+match" }
      ],
      "stdout": {
        "mode": "summary",
        "lines": [
          { "match": "^summary", "prefix": "  deadlines: " },
          { "match": "^calendar_sync", "prefix": "  calendar:  " }
        ]
      }
    },

    {
      "name": "mer-delivery-check",
      "schedule": "0 8,12,16 * * *",
      "schedule_human": "08:00, 12:00 and 16:00 daily (local)",
      "description": "Post-send bounce sweep. Read-only: reads the send ledger and the mailbox, sends nothing.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "Sending reports success the moment the mail server accepts the message for relay. A message",
        "can be accepted and bounce minutes later, and nothing in the engine ever looks back — so a",
        "dead escalation looks alive while its deadline clock keeps running. Three passes a day means",
        "a bounce surfaces within about four working hours; the overnight sends are caught at 08:00.",
        "Do not raise the frequency without lowering DELIVERY_CHECK_LOOKBACK_HOURS, or you will just",
        "re-report the same bounce more often."
      ],
      "env_from_file": ["MER_PROFILE", "MER_GOOGLE_TOKEN_FILE", "MER_SENDER", "MER_SENT_LEDGER"],
      "env": { "DELIVERY_CHECK_LOOKBACK_HOURS": "48" },
      "log": "delivery_check.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "delivery_check.py", "args": ["--cron"], "output": "alert" }
      ],
      "stdout": {
        "mode": "alert",
        "footer": "(read-only observation; nothing was re-sent)",
        "fail_rc_above": 1,
        "fail_message": "delivery_check exited {rc} with no output - the bounce sweep did not complete."
      }
    },

    {
      "name": "mer-sol-watchdog",
      "schedule": "0 10 * * 1",
      "schedule_human": "Mondays at 10:00 (local)",
      "description": "Statute-of-limitations sweep. Read-only: reads the board and prints, writes nothing.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "Every other deadline in the engine is our own. The statute of limitations is the OUTER clock,",
        "and nothing in the loop watches it: a case can be perfectly on schedule against every internal",
        "deadline and still cross the date after which no suit — and so no credible threat of suit — is",
        "possible. The limitations period moves exactly seven days per week, so a daily check would",
        "produce six identical answers and one useful one. Weekly is the correct sampling rate.",
        "Monday morning puts it at the start of a week with time left to act."
      ],
      "env_from_file": ["MULTICA_TOKEN", "MULTICA_WORKSPACE_ID", "MULTICA_SERVER_URL"],
      "env": { "SOL_ALERT_DAYS": "90" },
      "log": "sol_watchdog.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "sol_watchdog.py", "args": ["--cron"], "output": "alert" }
      ],
      "stdout": {
        "mode": "alert",
        "footer": "(record-only observation; nothing was written to the board)",
        "fail_rc_above": 0,
        "fail_message": "sol_watchdog exited {rc} with no output - the SoL sweep did not complete."
      }
    },

    {
      "name": "mer-unmatched-review",
      "schedule": "30 10 * * 1",
      "schedule_human": "Mondays at 10:30 (local)",
      "description": "Weekly missed-mail sweep: return-related inbound that matched no live case query. Read-only.",
      "why_on_a_clock": [
        "The engine matches inbound to a case by a per-case mailbox query. That is precise but narrow,",
        "and mail it does not match is mail the engine never sees at all — so no event inside the loop",
        "can ever trigger a review of it. Only a sweep over the whole inbox can. This is a slow leak,",
        "not an emergency, and a week's accumulation is small enough to read in one sitting, which is",
        "what makes it get read. The window is 8 days against a 7-day cadence: the extra day is",
        "deliberate overlap so a late or skipped run cannot open a hole.",
        "The 30-minute offset from the SoL sweep keeps two mailbox jobs off the same minute."
      ],
      "env_from_file": [
        "MULTICA_TOKEN",
        "MULTICA_WORKSPACE_ID",
        "MULTICA_SERVER_URL",
        "NANOGPT_API_KEY",
        "MINIMAX_API_KEY",
        "MER_PROFILE"
      ],
      "env": { "UNMATCHED_REVIEW_WINDOW": "newer_than:8d" },
      "log": "unmatched_review.log",
      "steps": [
        { "module": "unmatched_review.py", "args": ["--cron"], "output": "alert" }
      ],
      "stdout": {
        "mode": "alert",
        "footer": "(read-only observation; no case was opened and no mail was answered)",
        "fail_rc_above": 0,
        "fail_message": "unmatched_review exited {rc} with no output - the missed-mail sweep did not complete."
      }
    }
  ]
}
